#
# Original header, for ec/tools/disasm8051.py alone:
#
# ec/tools/disasm8051.py --self-test, wired into the cheap gate. Prepared here
# rather than landed, for the reason docs/ci/agent-gates-capture-claims.patch
# gives: `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh` is copied from the agent-pipeline
# template and the pipeline's push token has no `workflow` scope, so a branch
# editing it fails at the end of the PR rather than at the start of it.
# `docs/agent-pipeline.md` carries this one across a re-copy too, as its item 11.
#
# To land it:
#
#   git apply docs/ci/agent-gates-disasm8051-self-test.patch
#
# and that is the whole change.
#
# Why the cheap tier and not agent-gates-deep.sh: what the mode reads is the
# committed `ec/firmware/GMxMGxx_11.800`, the two hand transcriptions in
# `ec/annotations/`, and a hard-coded table. No Ghidra, no network, no
# assembler, and no `r2` either -- `r2 -a 8051` is in the *provenance* of those
# transcriptions, not in the run. So by the split `docs/agent-pipeline.md`
# records, it belongs beside `check_register_counts`, not in the deep tier. It
# measures 0.02 s here over five runs, against a cheap tier recorded at 5.9 s,
# on 2026-09-25. That is one runner's figure and the ratio is the point;
# item 6 gives the same caveat for `call_graph.py`.
#
# What it buys, and what it does not: `docs/findings.md:355` carries a
# load-bearing claim -- that no 8051 direct-addressing opcode takes a 16-bit
# operand, so a `0xFFxx` value "**cannot** be a direct address whatever
# anything spelled it as" -- and says of the table behind it that "its own
# `--self-test` pins" it. That mode ran in no gate, in either tier, and
# `grep -rn "disasm8051.py --self-test" .github/` returns nothing. The same
# goes for the r2 transcriptions `ec/tools/test_disasm8051.py:3-6` names as
# "the oracle for the opcode and mnemonic tables". **Until a human lands this,
# both tables can change in an otherwise-green PR.**
#
# Five groups, not the two the issue lists: 18 instructions across the two
# `charge-profile-flow.md` windows diffed byte for byte against the hand
# transcriptions; 4 `REL_SITES` (two backward branches the windows contain none
# of, and 0xFE24 as the last-byte-not-second-byte check); 11 `BIT_SITES`; the
# `0xC1`/`0xC2` `TEXTBOOK_BIT_SITES` pair, stated from the manual rather than
# transcribed, which exists because an oracle derived from the tool under test
# asserts nothing; and the `paged_target()` page-rule edge (`01 ff` at 0x07FE
# -> 0x08FF). 36 assertions in all.
#
# What it does not do, so the `--self-test` line in the landed script is not
# read as more than it is. (An earlier cut of this patch carried this as a
# comment at that call site; the substance is here instead, because a reader of
# the landed `agent-gates.sh` should not have to cut this patch to find it --
# re-adding it there is a separate, deliberate change to that template copy.)
# It is **not** a disassembler check. It covers 18 of OPCODE_LEN's 256
# entries by transference, so a row change outside those windows is not caught.
# It says nothing about `decode()`'s bounds contract, which has its own suite
# at `ec/tools/test_disasm8051.py` that `tools/run-tests.sh` collects and **no
# gate calls** -- so both halves of this decoder's own testing are ungated
# today, in two different ways. And it arbitrates none of the 0xA0/0xB0
# `ANL C,/bit` / `ORL C,/bit` disagreement that disasm8051.py:176-186 leaves
# open on purpose, where no committed instruction is affected either way. It
# reads a committed image, a committed annotation file and a hard-coded table:
# no capture is opened, no EC is opened, no register is read back, and nothing
# here is evidence about the laptop.
#
# The three `ec/tools/` entries sit at the *end* of the `for tool in` list,
# after `windows/tools/decompile_native.py` and so not grouped with the other
# `ec/tools/` paths; the `case` arms sit after `*xdata_register_map.py)`
# and so not beside any semantic neighbour (`*citation_gap_scan.py)` for
# disasm8051, and none at all for the others). All of them are
# deliberate and none is where a reader would put them.
# The second tool's own `case` arm runs `--check` as well as `--self-test`,
# unlike the first's, because `data_regions.py --check` is the mode that
# actually holds `annotations/data-regions.yaml` to the committed image: every
# span listed there, each re-derived for stride, entry count and first/last
# value, with a refusal for an entry whose `evidence` is empty or whose
# `confidence` or `shape` is outside its vocabulary. Its `--self-test` holds
# those refusals and an oracle transcribed from
# `annotations/bank-call-audit.md` rather than from
# the YAML, so the tool cannot grade its own homework. It reads two committed
# files: no Ghidra, no network, no assembler, no `r2`, no capture, no EC.
# Nothing about it is a behavioural claim and no `status:` in
# registers.yaml moves, which is why it belongs in the cheap tier beside
# check_register_counts rather than anywhere deeper.
#
# dsdt_ec_fields.py's arm runs `--csv --check` and `--self-test`, and is the only
# one that passes an argument: `ECMG` is in `XDATA_REGIONS`, so
# its three `static_refs*` columns are functions of the image and both
# `--check` and the bare table refuse to run without one. The path is relative
# to the repo root the way every other path in this script is.
# `--check` is the half that matters here, and it is the mode the issue was
# filed about: it is what holds `ec/annotations/dsdt-ecmg-fields.csv` to
# `evidence/acpi/dsdt.dsl` and the committed image, so the `in_registers` and
# `grade` columns cannot drift as `registers.yaml` moves -- and the write-up's
# own §2 records that both of those summary figures are functions of *when* the
# tool runs, which is the whole reason `--check` exists. Its `--self-test` pins
# the bit arithmetic against oracles from opposite ends of the stack
# (`registers.yaml` and `windows/tools/gpu_block_watch.py`) and the six parser
# refusals, so a check that has quietly stopped refusing still looks exactly
# like a check that is working. It reads four committed files -- the image, the
# DSDT, `registers.yaml` and the committed CSV -- so no Ghidra, no network, no
# assembler, no `r2`, no capture and no EC, and nothing in them is evidence
# about the laptop: the 13 `present-untested` and 3 `unknown-not-absent` rows
# the sweep landed stay untested, and a register write being accepted is not
# evidence the EC acts on it. It measures 0.74 s for `--csv --check` and 0.16 s
# for `--self-test` here over three runs on 2026-09-27, against a cheap tier
# recorded at 5.9 s -- one runner's figures, and the ratio is the point, as
# item 6 says for `call_graph.py`; the two commands are the two lines of the
# arm, so a reader re-derives them rather than quoting this sentence.
# **Until a human lands this, both modes run by hand and a CSV that has drifted
# from `registers.yaml` still merges green.**
#
# **Where the two hunks are cut, and why not somewhere tidier.** The reason is
# `tools/test_agent_gates_patches.py`, which applies the set in **every
# ordered pair** against a scratch copy of the committed script. Two hunks
# cutting one contiguous region cannot both be independently applicable --
# whichever lands first inserts a line between the other's leading and
# trailing context -- so the placements are the ones that leave each of this
# patch's two hunks outside the other four patches' context windows:
#
#   tool list :119-124  held by agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch hunk 1
#   tool list :123-128  held by agent-gates-0751-self-test.patch hunk 1
#   tool list :141-147  this patch's hunk 1
#   arms      :163-168  held by agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch hunk 2
#   arms      :190-195  held by agent-gates-0751-self-test.patch hunk 2
#   arms      :152-157  this patch's hunk 2
#
# (Content-matched against the committed script rather than read off the `@@`
# numbers, which are stale for most of this set; that is what the first two
# rows and the last two were measured with. The arms rows were one line low
# before the third tool was folded in and are corrected here.)
#
# The union of the two tool-list windows this patch was cut against is :119-128,
# and :129 -- the `; do` line this hunk splits -- was the only line in the list
# outside every one of them. Adding this patch's own window makes the union of
# them all :119-147: the whole list, the `[ -f "$tool" ] || continue` guard and
# the `case "$tool" in` line. **There is no free boundary left in the tool list
# for another `ec/tools/` entry**, which is the whole reason the later tools
# fold into an existing hunk's post-image -- the pre-images are untouched, so
# the spans above are still the spans, and every other patch still composes.
# The arm's is the same shape at the other end of the arm list. List order here
# is cosmetic: nothing reads the order, the loop is over a `for tool in` word
# list. **"Tidying" either placement back to where a reader expects it breaks
# every-ordered-pair landing**, and the failure is silent in the sense that
# matters -- each patch still applies cleanly alone. That is the same class of
# note the 0751 header carries about its own re-cut, and
# `docs/findings/prepared-gate-patches.md` is where a future re-cut should
# start.

